 over time whereas among  boys the boys who had these change in  aspirations were really not as large  so where was this coming from  interestingly it was coming from a very  important decrease in the number of  girls who aspired to be office workers  in the 1980s one in five girls wanted to  be a secretary or an office worker  and then in the  that was 1 in 40. that's a tremendous  change that makes a lot of sense from a  labor economy's point of view because  essentially uh these the demand for  these occupation has declined  tremendously over time with the  increases in computers in the office and  so on  over that period of time there was also  some decline in the expectations of boys  wanting to work in the what we could  call middle skill occupation  craftsmen and so on but not to the same  extent  so  then we did a more detailed analysis to  try to compare  this  these change in plans for the future to  other factors that had also changed over  time there there was a student behavior  the family environment and work during  school over that 35 years period  there had been changes on gender  differences and what we found is that  the idea of plan for the future was the  overwhelming factor that was explaining  for example there is an excess a six  percentage point in the number of  girls who are earning a's and about a  third of that was actually accounted by  the gender differences in these plan for  the future  and also importantly  this was also happening among our 8th  graders  so our 8th graders here we had better  measures of student behavior which  included misbehavior having been sent to  the office the the the year prior and so  on  and so here sure enough uh we could  explain some of the excess girl in da's  with  these plans for the future  at about the same the same percentage  but some part of the d the excess of  boys in the c's and the d's was also  explained by uh student misbehavior so  um these were  very interesting findings that we're  going to probe  by going at  lower level schools and this was done  with  observational data  as economists when we want to take the  long view we go to observational data  but i want to say that these findings  have been incorporated in some  experimental research this one in  particular conducted by srdc  where they had some experimental  intervention trying to  improve access to post-secondary  education and they did find much larger  differences much larger impact of their  intervention for boys and for girls  essentially the girls were already on  track  so the big question  why should we care about this new gender  imbalance in higher education  it shouldn't be as we say in french  just all day shows a proper reversal of  roles  um  well let's adopt different perspective  as we're  now doing  in the context of cfar  let's think of the economist the  economists will care about the  misallocation of resources that could be  due to misinformation perhaps the young  boys are  unaware of the wages and employment  opportunity in the mid-scale occupations  perhaps the young women are unaware of  the high demand of the professions that  they want to embrace  so that could create some skill mismatch  let's now think about a demographer the  demographer may be  concerned about marriage markets  with  less  men getting college education  more college educated women will marry  men with lower level of education  this might leave  less educated women unmarried and  raising families in more difficult  circumstances  now the sociologists may care about the  change in the balance of power in the  family and so this is uh from the  canadian data actually  uh and  from the work of marianne bertrand  cooper where she shows that  when the wife  shares of total family income exceeds  one half there is an increased risk of  divorce  so really kind of interesting um  issues here uh will wives want to  curtail their hours in the labor market  to reduce their hours and their um  avoid overtaking their husband's earning  all kinds of very interesting questions  and finally for the parents  the parents may be concerned that their  girls are going to be perhaps stretched  out between the demands of the career  and the demand of the family the parents  of boys may be concerned about  boys spending too much time playing  video games and so on as the male  picture suggests whereas they would  like to see their boy becoming  businessmen and so on so these are all a  very important question and especially i  think the questions of the role of video  gaming is one that  i will want to address  in future research  however all of this is not uh so dire  because when we look at happiness all  things considered here it's from a 2.3  point scale we see that over time  basically the gender gap in happiness  has reverse and the boys are relatively  more happy  than the girls  so i will conclude with this thank you  very much  you
 

